Step 0: Set Up Real-Time Observation
Open a second terminal window (keep it side-by-side with your working shell) and start a live watch on the namespace:

Bash
kubectl get pods -n mini-project-1 -w
Keep this running throughout the experiments so you can observe the status transitions in real time.


Test 1: Simulating Pod Deletion (Deployment Controller Reconciliation)

In this test, you delete a healthy pod. The underlying controller immediately detects a discrepancy between the desired state (replicas: 2) and actual state (replicas: 1).

1.Identify a target frontend pod:

Get the name of one of your running frontend pods:

Bash
TARGET_POD=$(kubectl get pods -n mini-project-1 -l app=frontend -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
echo "Target pod to delete: $TARGET_POD"

2.Delete the pod:

Issue the delete command:

Bash

kubectl delete pod $TARGET_POD -n mini-project-1

3.Observe the watcher terminal:

Notice the lifecycle events in your watch terminal:

The target pod enters Terminating.
Simultaneously, a new pod with a fresh random hash suffix transitions: Pending → ContainerCreating → Running.Check the final state:

Bash

kubectl get pods -n mini-project-1

Result: Total frontend count never drops below 2 healthy pods.

Test 2: Process Crash via SIGKILL (Kubelet Restarts Container)

Here, we simulate an unhandled application crash or Out-Of-Memory kill where the main container process (PID 1) dies suddenly. The pod itself is not deleted—the node's kubelet detects the process exit and enforces the pod's restartPolicy: Always.

1.Pick a frontend pod and check its current restart count:

Bash

TARGET_POD=$(kubectl get pods -n mini-project-1 -l app=frontend -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
kubectl get pod $TARGET_POD -n mini-project-1

Notice: RESTARTS is currently 0.

2.Send SIGKILL (kill -9) to process 1 inside the container:

Bash
kubectl exec -n mini-project-1 $TARGET_POD -- kill 1

(The command will exit with an error code like command terminated with exit code 137, which is expected when killing PID 1).

3.Verify the self-healing restart:

Check the pod status immediately:

Bash
kubectl get pod $TARGET_POD -n mini-project-1

Result:
The pod name remains the same (it was not rescheduled to a new pod).
STATUS is back to Running.
RESTARTS has incremented to 1.
The kubelet restarted the container in less than 2 seconds.

Test 3: Liveness Probe Failure (Automated Recovery from Deadlock)

A process might still be running (no crash), but stuck in a deadlock or unresponsive to traffic. This is where Liveness Probes trigger automated intervention.

1.Target the Redis pod:

Bash
REDIS_POD=$(kubectl get pods -n mini-project-1 -l app=redis -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
echo "Redis pod: $REDIS_POD"

2.Shut down the Redis server process directly:
Simulate Redis becoming unresponsive without killing the container itself:

Bash
kubectl exec -n mini-project-1 $REDIS_POD -- redis-cli -a SuperSecureK8sPassword123! shutdown

3.Observe probe failure and kubelet intervention:

Watch the pod:

Bash
kubectl get pod $REDIS_POD -n mini-project-1 -w

Within 5–10 seconds (as configured in periodSeconds: 10):

The TCP probe to port 6379 fails.
Inspect the events log:

Bash
kubectl describe pod $REDIS_POD -n mini-project-1 | grep -A 5 -B 2 "Liveness probe failed"

Result: The kubelet kills the unresponsive container and starts a fresh Redis instance. RESTARTS increments to 1.

Key Takeaway: The Ephemeral Storage Trap

Now that you've watched the Redis container restart, notice what happened to the data:
Because Redis was running as a standard Deployment with no persistent volume mounted, any data or hit counter values stored in memory or local disk before the restart were permanently wiped.

Let's upgrade Redis to a StatefulSet with a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) and test data survival across pod restarts.